'''Paris Saint-Germain Football Club''', commonly referred to as '''Paris Saint-Germain''' ({{IPA|fr|paʁi sɛ̃ʒɛʁmɛ̃|lang}},  {{tooltip|ult. ref.|ultimately referring to}} "Saint [[Germain of Paris|Germanus]]"), '''PSG''', '''Paris''', or '''Paris SG''', is a French professional [[Association football|football]] club based in [[Paris]]. Founded in 1970 through the merger of [[Paris FC]] and [[Stade Saint-Germain]], the club competes in [[Ligue 1]], the [[French football league system|highest division]] of [[Football in France|French football]], and plays its home matches at the [[Parc des Princes]]. PSG is France's most widely supported football club and one of the most followed clubs worldwide. The club has rivalries with [[Olympique de Marseille]], against whom it contests [[Le Classique]], and Paris FC in the [[Paris derby]].

PSG is the [[Football records and statistics in France|most successful club in French football]] and rank among the leading clubs worldwide by [[List of football clubs by competitive honours won|number of competitive trophies won]]. Domestically, the club have won a record 14 Ligue 1 titles, one [[Ligue 2]] title, 16 Coupes de France, 9 [[Coupe de la Ligue|Coupes de la Ligue]], and 14 [[Trophée des Champions]]. PSG is also the most decorated French club internationally, having won two [[UEFA Champions League]] and two [[UEFA Super Cup]] titles, as well as a [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup]], a [[UEFA Intertoto Cup]], and a [[FIFA Intercontinental Cup]] title, all French records.

Before the club's takeover by [[Qatar Sports Investments]] (QSI) in 2011, PSG had won 18 trophies, including two European titles, two Ligue 1 championships and 13 [[Football in France#Cup competitions|national cups]], placing among the top five in French football at the time. Following QSI's acquisition of the club, the club entered a period of sustained domestic dominance, attracting numerous high-profile players and becoming the country's most successful club. Under manager [[Luis Enrique]], PSG also established itself as a dominant force in international football, winning consecutive UEFA Champions League and UEFA Super Cup titles, the FIFA Intercontinental Cup, as well as a [[Treble (association football)|continental treble]] and [[Sextuple (association football)|sextuple]] in [[2024–25 Paris Saint-Germain FC season|2025]].

Since 2011, PSG have been majority-owned by QSI, with [[Arctos Partners]] acquiring a minority stake in 2023 and [[Boardroom (company)|Boardroom]] in 2025. As of May 2026, the club is the wealthiest in France and ranks among the [[Deloitte Football Money League|richest]] and 
[[Forbes' list of the most valuable football clubs|most valuable football clubs in the world]]. In addition to its men's football team, PSG operates a [[Paris Saint-Germain FC Youth Academy|youth academy]], a [[Paris Saint-Germain FC (women)|women's football team]], and sections in [[Paris Saint-Germain Handball|handball]], [[Paris Saint-Germain Judo|judo]], and [[PSG Esports|esports]]. The club has also previously fielded teams in [[Paris Saint-Germain Basketball|basketball]], [[Paris Saint-Germain Volleyball|volleyball]], [[Paris Saint-Germain Boxing|boxing]], and [[Paris Saint-Germain Rugby League|rugby league]]."}}
